```csharp
// Record (C# 9+) — immutable by default, value equality
public record Point(double X, double Y) {
    public double Distance(Point other) =>
        Math.Sqrt(Math.Pow(X-other.X, 2) + Math.Pow(Y-other.Y, 2));
}

// Value equality (not reference)
var p1 = new Point(3, 4);
var p2 = new Point(3, 4);
Console.WriteLine(p1 == p2);  // true! (class would be false)

// Non-destructive mutation with 'with'
var p3 = p1 with { X = 0 };   // new Point(0, 4)

// record struct (C# 10+) — value type record
public record struct Size(double Width, double Height);
```

Use `record` when:
- Data is immutable or rarely mutated
- You want value-based equality (compare by content, not reference)
- You need non-destructive mutation patterns

